{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Posidonia Meadow: Sulfide Ambush in a Seagrass Rhizosphere",
    "date": "2026-11-14",
    "lesson": "Mediterranean Posidonia oceanica seagrass meadows pump oxygen into their roots, but the surrounding carbonate sediment is organically rich and anoxic just millimeters away. Sulfate-reducing bacteria thrive in that anoxic mud, generating sulfide that is toxic to the seagrass roots if it leaks past the oxidized rhizosphere boundary. A thin community of sulfide-oxidizing bacteria lives at that boundary, intercepting the sulfide before it reaches the roots - a buffer zone that collapses if organic loading (from summer die-off of leaf litter) overwhelms it. This level puts you at that knife-edge boundary: carbohydrate-rich leaf detritus feeds fermenters, protein-rich root exudate feeds another guild, and the sulfide plume rising from below is both a chemical energy source and a slow poison to anything that strays too deep without the right chemistry.",
    "citation": "Garcias-Bonet et al. 2008, Marba & Duarte seagrass sediment biogeochemistry studies",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Posidonia oceanica meadow rhizosphere sulfide cycling, NW Mediterranean (e.g. Mallorca coastal meadows)"
  },
